  "name": "Rake-and-scrape (Bahamian saw-and-accordion dance music)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Out Island dance music of the Bahamas, strongest on Cat Island and Long Island, played on a carpenter's handsaw bent and scraped with a screwdriver or knife, a goatskin goombay drum and a button accordion, sometimes with guitar. It accompanied quadrilles and other social dances and is celebrated at an annual festival on Cat Island; the scraper card stands in for the saw and the hand-drum card for the goatskin drum.",
  "recipe": "classic modern rake-and-scrape, groove Percussion, Caribbean branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment, single lead single track no stacking. accordion, guayo source hand drum source unspecified. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
